# Status

This is the proof spine that now looks correct.

It is not yet an unconditional theorem of the whole MTT corpus.  It is a
conditional theorem whose assumptions are exactly the missing lemmas the papers
must now prove.

The point is to replace the vague target:

```text
find Z_448 in the topology
```

by the precise target:

```text
prove that the fixed arithmetic coherent sector selects a unitary CP character
of order 448.
```

# Corpus support

The required fixed-sector language is already present in the corpus.

The Strominger/heterotic selection paper treats the B-field as a Deligne
2-gerbe connection, takes local variations inside a fixed differential
cohomology class, and explicitly fixes a topological sector:

```text
Chern data,
cohomology class of Hhat compatible with the Bianchi identity.
```

It also states the selection theorem in a fixed topological sector.

The heterotic flux paper supplies the Lens x Nil invariant component system:

```text
left-invariant truncation,
componentwise Bianchi equations,
integer flux labels,
integral periods,
globally well-defined gerbe.
```

The Kaluza-Klein/FCC paper states the relevant reduction in the right
arithmetic form:

```text
left-invariant equations reduce to a finite algebraic/Diophantine system
in continuous parameters and integer flux/holonomy data.
```

The central-circle paper supplies the family factor:

```text
flavor line bundle over the central circle,
finite U(1) holonomy,
family sectors Z_3.
```

# Correct objects

Fix a topological/differential-cohomology sector:

```text
T.
```

Let:

```text
Lambda_T,Z
```

be the integral Bianchi/gerbe/character lattice in that sector.  In the
Lens-Nil invariant block, take a rank-two arithmetic coherent lattice:

```text
Lambda_LN,Z subset Lambda_T,Z.
```

Important caution:

```text
Lambda_LN,Z is not the naive de Rham H^4 lattice generated by beta_1,beta_3.
```

The closure check shows that the individual invariant forms `beta_1,beta_3`
are component forms, not closed de Rham generators.  The right object is the
integral Bianchi/gerbe component lattice selected in the fixed sector.

The analytic coherent projector should be regarded as scalar extension:

```text
Lambda_LN,Z  ->  Lambda_LN,Z tensor R  ->  Ran(Pi_coh).
```

That is the arithmetic addendum needed by the fixed-point papers.

# Lens-Nil block

The Lens x Nil coefficient appendix gives:

```text
dH = W_1 beta_1 + W_3 beta_3,
W_1 = 2 lambda^2 R^2,
W_3 = lambda nu R^2,
```

and:

```text
Tr_grav R_+^2 = A beta_1 + B beta_3,
A = 4 lambda^2 + O(lambda^2 nu^2),
B = 4 nu^2     + O(lambda^2 nu^2).
```

So the visible reduced integer fingerprint is:

```text
K_LN =
[2 1
 1 4].
```

The `O(lambda^2 nu^2)` terms are not harmless by default.  The proof must show
one of the following:

```text
1. these terms vanish in the relevant integral character projection;
2. they are absorbed into continuous metric/radius equations and do not alter
   the integer relation matrix;
3. the exact differential-cohomology relation matrix is GL(2,Z)-equivalent to
   [[2,1],[1,4]] in the fixed sector.
```

This is the integer-block protection lemma.

# Theorem A: sevenfold arithmetic descent

Assume:

```text
1. Lambda_LN,Z is a rank-two fixed arithmetic coherent lattice.
2. The family-trivial residual CP labels w,n are unitary characters of
   Lambda_LN,Z / K_LN Lambda_LN,Z.
3. In this lattice, the exact relation matrix is
   K_LN = [[2,1],[1,4]]
   up to GL(2,Z) row and column basis changes.
```

Then:

```text
Gamma_7 := Hom(Lambda_LN,Z / K_LN Lambda_LN,Z, U(1)) ~= Z_7.
```

Proof:

```text
det(K_LN) = 2*4 - 1*1 = 7.
gcd(2,1,1,4) = 1.
```

Hence the Smith normal form of `K_LN` is:

```text
[7].
```

The quotient lattice is cyclic of order seven.  Finite abelian groups are
canonically Pontryagin-dual to their unitary character groups up to noncanonical
choice of generator, so:

```text
Hom(coker K_LN, U(1)) ~= Z_7.
```

Equivalently, if:

```text
2w + n  = 0,
w  + 4n = 0,
```

then elimination gives:

```text
7w = 0,
7n = 0.
```

Thus the seven is derived as a determinant, not inserted as a primitive row.

# Theorem B: selected CP quotient

Assume also that the six-stage shared-circle carry block is exact:

```text
2x_0 - x_1 = 0,
2x_1 - x_2 = 0,
2x_2 - x_3 = 0,
2x_3 - x_4 = 0,
2x_4 - x_5 = 0,
2x_5       = 0.
```

Its Smith normal form is:

```text
[64].
```

Since:

```text
gcd(64,7)=1,
```

the product is cyclic:

```text
Z_64 x Z_7 ~= Z_448.
```

Therefore the minimal selected CP quotient is:

```text
Gamma_CP,min ~= Z_448.
```

# Theorem C: ambient family quotient

The central-circle family sector contributes:

```text
Z_3.
```

If it is carried in the same ambient finite carrier, then:

```text
Gamma_amb ~= Z_64 x Z_7 x Z_3 ~= Z_1344.
```

The selected CP quotient is obtained by removing the family kernel:

```text
Gamma_amb / Z_3-family ~= Z_448.
```

Equivalently, in the cyclic ambient presentation:

```text
pi: Z_1344 -> Z_448,
pi(x) = x mod 448,
ker(pi) = {0,448,896}.
```

The CKM CP label:

```text
k_q = 237 in Z_1344
```

has:

```text
gcd(237,1344)=3,
order = 1344/3 = 448.
```

So the selected CP character can have order 448 even when the ambient carrier
has order 1344.

# What this proves

Under the stated arithmetic descent assumptions, it proves:

```text
MTT selects a finite unitary CP character of order 448.
```

It does not prove:

```text
the entire topology is Z_448,
the full flavor carrier has no extra factors,
or the number 448 appears as a fundamental dimension.
```

The achievement is sharper:

```text
448 becomes the order of a selected CP character in a fixed arithmetic coherent
sector.
```

# Remaining proof obligations

Only three serious obligations remain.

1. Dyadic carry derivation:

```text
derive the six carry rows from recursive shared-circle/proto-spinor refinement.
```

2. Lens-Nil character-dual descent:

```text
prove that residual CP labels w,n are the unitary dual characters of
Lambda_LN,Z / K_LN Lambda_LN,Z.
```

3. Integer-block protection:

```text
prove that higher-order analytic corrections and the O(lambda^2 nu^2) curvature
terms do not change the exact integral relation matrix within the fixed sector.
```

This is now the correct way forward: prove these three lemmas, not search the
corpus for a literal `Z_448` statement.

